{title: Danny Boy}
{artist: Frederic Weatherly, 1913}
{key: C}
{tempo: 70}
{difficulty: intermediate}
{tags: folk, ballad, irish}

{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
Oh Danny [C]boy, the [C7]pipes, the [F]pipes are [C]calling
From glen to [Am]glen, and [D7]down the mountain [G]side[G7]
The [C]summer's [C7]gone, and [F]all the roses [C]falling
It's [C]you, it's [Am]you must [G]go and [G7]I must [C]bide
But come ye [F]back when [G]summer's in the [C]meadow
Or [Am]when the valley's [F]hushed and white with [G]snow[G7]
It's [C]I'll be [C7]here in [F]sunshine or in [C]shadow
Oh Danny [Am]boy, oh [F]Danny [G]boy, I love you [C]so

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
But when ye [C]come, and [C7]all the [F]flowers are [C]dying
If I am [Am]dead, as [D7]dead I well may [G]be[G7]
Ye'll [C]come and [C7]find the [F]place where I am [C]lying
And [C]kneel and [Am]say an [G]Ave [G7]there for [C]me
And I shall [F]hear, though [G]soft you tread a[C]bove me
And [Am]all my grave will [F]warmer, sweeter [G]be[G7]
For [C]you will [C7]bend and [F]tell me that you [C]love me
And I shall [Am]sleep in [F]peace un[G]til you come to [C]me
